@page "/help"

<PageTitle>Help - RaceTrade</PageTitle>

<div class="page-head">
    <h1><Icon Name="help" Size="18" />Help</h1>
</div>

<div class="doc-page">
    <section class="doc-hero">
        <div>
            <span class="doc-kicker">RaceTrade WebUI</span>
            <h2>Correct guide for the new web build</h2>
            <p>
                RaceTrade is now one executable: the racing engine runs inside the same
                process as this local web UI. You configure it in the browser, but your
                sites, cbftp servers, prebots, sections and databases are still normal files
                in the data folder shown under Settings.
            </p>
        </div>
        <a class="btn primary doc-hero-action" href="settings"><Icon Name="settings" Size="13" /><span>Settings</span></a>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-grid">
        <article class="doc-card">
            <div class="card-title">QUICK START</div>
            <ol class="doc-steps">
                <li>Add a CBFTP server under <a href="servers">CBFTP servers</a> and press Save.</li>
                <li>Use <strong>Import sites from CBFTP</strong> to fetch site names and section mappings.</li>
                <li>Open each imported site under <a href="sites">Sites</a> and fill ZNC, bot and channel details.</li>
                <li>In the Sections tab, enable the IRC sections that should race.</li>
                <li>Use <a href="test">Test release</a> before starting the trader.</li>
                <li>Use <a href="fxp">FXP Client</a> for manual CBFTP browsing and FXP jobs.</li>
                <li>Press Start on the left when the configuration validates.</li>
            </ol>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
            <div class="card-title">DATA FOLDER</div>
            <p>
                The engine reads relative folders such as <code>sites</code>, <code>cbftp</code>,
                <code>pre_bots</code>, <code>sections</code>, <code>settings</code> and <code>db</code>
                from the configured data folder.
            </p>
            <p>
                Do not guess the folder location. Open Settings and check <strong>Data folder</strong>.
                Copy old WinForms configs into that folder if you are migrating.
            </p>
            <p>
                Old WinForms files can contain Windows DPAPI <code>ENC:</code> secrets.
                On the same Windows account that created them, run
                <code>RaceTrade.exe --migrate-legacy-secrets --data C:\Path\To\Data</code>
                once before moving the data to another machine or Linux.
            </p>
        </article>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>Port, remote access and SSL</h2>
        <p>
            Open <strong>Settings</strong> and use <strong>WebUI access</strong> to change the
            WebUI port, bind address or data folder. Save the values, then quit and start
            RaceTrade again. The app reads those startup settings before the engine starts.
        </p>
        <ul class="doc-list">
            <li><code>--port 8421</code> starts another RaceTrade copy on a different WebUI port.</li>
            <li><code>--bind 127.0.0.1</code> keeps it local-only, which is the default.</li>
            <li><code>--bind 0.0.0.0</code> allows LAN/server access, but RaceTrade refuses this unless a password is set.</li>
            <li><code>--data D:\RaceTrade\data</code> points the engine at another data folder.</li>
            <li><code>--set-password</code> creates the WebUI login password hash.</li>
            <li><code>--migrate-legacy-secrets</code> converts old WinForms <code>ENC:</code> secrets to v2 <code>ENC2:</code> secrets.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            For public HTTPS, do not expose the RaceTrade port directly. Keep RaceTrade on
            <code>127.0.0.1</code> and put a reverse proxy such as Caddy in front of it.
            Caddy can handle Let's Encrypt certificates automatically:
        </p>
<pre class="code">racetrade.example.com {
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8420
}</pre>
        <p>
            For private remote access, a VPN, Tailscale/WireGuard or an SSH tunnel is usually
            cleaner than opening a port to the internet.
        </p>
        <p>
            Raspberry Pi 5 and other ARM64 Linux hosts use the <code>linux-arm64</code>
            release. Use a 64-bit OS, make <code>RaceTrade</code> executable, set a WebUI
            password, then run it with <code>--no-browser --bind 0.0.0.0</code> for LAN access.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>CBFTP servers and site import</h2>
        <p>
            The CBFTP servers page writes <code>cbftp/cbftp_config.json</code>. Passwords typed in
            the browser are encrypted before they reach disk.
        </p>
        <ul class="doc-list">
            <li><strong>Add server</strong> creates a new CBFTP API endpoint.</li>
            <li><strong>Save</strong> stores the server list and reloads the racer configuration.</li>
            <li><strong>Import sites from CBFTP</strong> fetches sites and sections from CBFTP.</li>
            <li>Imported sites include IRC section names, CBFTP mappings and enabled race sections.</li>
            <li>You still need to configure each site's ZNC host, network, channel and announce bot.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>Site editor tabs</h2>
        <div class="doc-grid compact">
            <article class="doc-card">
                <h3>General</h3>
                <p>Site name, bot nick, mode, max pretime and basic site flags.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="doc-card">
                <h3>ZNC</h3>
                <p>ZNC host, port, username, network and password. Network keeps joins on the right ZNC network.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="doc-card">
                <h3>Channels</h3>
                <p>IRC channels and optional Blowfish keys for this site's network.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="doc-card">
                <h3>Announce</h3>
                <p>Regex patterns and test buttons for detecting new releases, sections and release names.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="doc-card">
                <h3>Sections</h3>
                <p>Master-detail editor for routing IRC sections to CBFTP sections and filters.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="doc-card">
                <h3>Affils, Blacklist, Requests</h3>
                <p>Group download-only rules, wildcard skip patterns and request auto-fill settings.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="doc-card">
                <h3>CBFTP Site</h3>
                <p>Direct link to the CBFTP-side site editor and SITE RULES modal for the same sitename.</p>
            </article>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>Requests tab</h2>
        <p>
            Request auto-fill parses <code>SITE REQUESTS</code> output with your
            <strong>Request line regex</strong>. Use named groups <code>(?&lt;id&gt;)</code>,
            <code>(?&lt;name&gt;)</code> and optionally <code>(?&lt;user&gt;)</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Fill command template</strong> supports <code>{id}</code> and
            <code>{name}</code>. <strong>Dst path template</strong> supports
            <code>{id}</code>, <code>{name}</code>, <code>{user}</code>,
            <code>{release}</code> and <code>{sitename}</code>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>CBFTP Site editor</h2>
        <p>
            RaceTrade site configs and CBFTP site configs are different things. The <strong>Sites</strong>
            list has <strong>Edit CBFTP Site</strong>, and each site editor has a <strong>CBFTP Site</strong>
            tab. Use it for CBFTP addresses, login, base path, transfer flags, affils, section paths and skiplist.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Get Site Rules</strong> sends <code>SITE RULES</code> to CBFTP and shows the result in a modal.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>Sections tab</h2>
        <p>
            Sections are now shown as a list on the left and a single editor on the right.
            This page replaces the old WinForms Race Sections and Mapping area.
        </p>
        <ol class="doc-steps">
            <li>Select the IRC section on the left.</li>
            <li>Tick <strong>Enabled for racing</strong>, or click the <strong>Racing/Off</strong> label in the left list.</li>
            <li>Add one or more CBFTP mappings. Empty trigger regex means fallback/default.</li>
            <li>Add <strong>CBFTP Mapping Rules</strong> under a mapping when only that CBFTP target needs extra filtering.</li>
            <li>Add <strong>Section Rules</strong> when every mapping in this IRC section should use the same filter.</li>
            <li>Enable IMDB or TVMaze filters only when that section needs lookup checks; their genre, network and show type lists are under <strong>Lookup filters</strong>.</li>
            <li>Save the site after changing routing or filters.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Core terms: <strong>IRC section</strong> is what the announce says, for example
            <code>TV-DE</code> or <code>X264-HD</code>. <strong>CBFTP section</strong> is the shared
            section name sent to CBFTP. <strong>Mapping</strong> is the link between them plus an
            optional trigger regex and mapping rules.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>Section routing and mappings</h2>
        <p>
            When an announce arrives, RaceTrade parses the release name and IRC section,
            checks that the IRC section is enabled, then picks a CBFTP mapping from the
            selected site's Sections tab.
        </p>
        <ol class="doc-steps">
            <li>RaceTrade strips the configured section prefix/suffix, for example <code>[</code> and <code>]</code>.</li>
            <li>It finds the matching IRC section in the site config.</li>
            <li>It walks the mappings for that IRC section in order.</li>
            <li>A mapping with a trigger regex is only considered when the regex matches the release name.</li>
            <li>A mapping with an empty trigger acts as the default/catch-all mapping; keep it after more specific mappings.</li>
            <li>The first mapping that is allowed by its trigger and rules becomes the CBFTP section for the race.</li>
        </ol>
        <h3>Trigger regex</h3>
        <ul class="doc-list">
            <li>
                It is tested against the <strong>release name only</strong>. Not the whole
                announce line, not the section, not the group on its own.
            </li>
            <li>
                It is a <strong>search, not a full match</strong>: the pattern only has to
                occur somewhere in the name. <code>1080p</code> matches
                <code>Some.Show.S01E01.1080p.WEB.H264-GRP</code>. You do not need
                <code>.*</code> around it.
            </li>
            <li>
                It is <strong>case sensitive</strong> by default. Write it as
                <code>/1080p/i</code> to ignore case. The <code>/pattern/flags</code> form is
                the only place where <code>/</code> is stripped; a bare pattern is used exactly
                as typed.
            </li>
            <li>
                Standard .NET regex syntax: <code>|</code> is OR, <code>\b</code> is a word
                boundary, <code>\.</code> is a literal dot.
            </li>
            <li>
                An <strong>empty</strong> trigger is the catch-all. Because mappings are
                walked in order, an empty trigger anywhere but last makes every mapping under
                it unreachable.
            </li>
            <li>
                An <strong>invalid</strong> pattern is logged as an error and that mapping is
                skipped, so the release quietly falls through to the next mapping.
            </li>
        </ul>
<pre class="code">Release: Some.Show.S01E01.2160p.WEB.DV.HEVC-GRP
IRC section: TV

Mapping 1: CBFTP TV-2160P  Trigger: 2160p|UHD|HEVC   -> matches, race goes here
Mapping 2: CBFTP TV-1080P  Trigger: /1080p|x264/i    -> not reached
Mapping 3: CBFTP TV        Trigger: (empty)          -> not reached

Useful patterns
2160p|UHD|HEVC        one of these words appears
/x265/i               x265 or X265 or x265 in any casing
\bMULTi\b             MULTi as a whole word, not MULTiSUBS
-(GRP1|GRP2)$         release from one of these groups
FRENCH|GERMAN|ITA     foreign language releases</pre>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>Rule format</h2>
        <p>
            Section Rules and CBFTP Mapping Rules use the same syntax:
        </p>
<pre class="code">[key] operator value ACTION</pre>
        <ul class="doc-list">
            <li><code>[key]</code> is the field to inspect, such as <code>[release]</code> or <code>[group]</code>.</li>
            <li><code>operator</code> is the comparison type.</li>
            <li><code>value</code> is the value, list, wildcard or regex to compare with.</li>
            <li><code>ACTION</code> is <code>DROP</code>, <code>ALLOW</code> or <code>EXCEPT</code>. If omitted, the engine treats it as <code>ALLOW</code>; write it explicitly for clarity.</li>
        </ul>
<pre class="code">[release] contains INTERNAL DROP
[group] isin BadGroup1,BadGroup2 DROP
[release] contains 1080p ALLOW
[group] isin APPROVED1,APPROVED2 EXCEPT</pre>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>Rule operators</h2>
        <div class="doc-grid compact">
            <article class="doc-card">
                <h3>String checks</h3>
                <ul class="doc-list">
                    <li><code>==</code> exact match, case-insensitive.</li>
                    <li><code>!=</code> not equal, case-insensitive.</li>
                    <li><code>contains</code> substring exists.</li>
                    <li><code>startswith</code> begins with text.</li>
                    <li><code>endswith</code> ends with text.</li>
                </ul>
            </article>
            <article class="doc-card">
                <h3>Lists and patterns</h3>
                <ul class="doc-list">
                    <li><code>isin</code> exact membership in a comma or pipe separated list.</li>
                    <li><code>iswm</code> wildcard match with <code>*</code> and <code>?</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>matches</code> regex match. Regex is evaluated case-insensitive with a safety timeout.</li>
                </ul>
            </article>
        </div>
        <p>
            <code>isin</code> is exact membership, not substring matching:
            <code>[group] isin GRP1,GRP2</code> matches <code>GRP1</code>, but not
            <code>MYGRP1</code>. Use <code>contains</code> or <code>matches</code> for substring
            behaviour.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>Rule keys</h2>
        <p>
            Rules only match when the key exists for that release. If a key cannot be
            parsed from the release name, that rule is skipped.
        </p>
        <div class="doc-grid compact">
            <article class="doc-card">
                <h3>Always useful</h3>
                <ul class="doc-list">
                    <li><code>[release]</code> full release name.</li>
                    <li><code>[section]</code> IRC section name after prefix/suffix stripping.</li>
                    <li><code>[group]</code> release group when parsed from the final dash.</li>
                    <li><code>[year]</code> movie/show year when parsed.</li>
                </ul>
            </article>
            <article class="doc-card">
                <h3>Media metadata</h3>
                <ul class="doc-list">
                    <li><code>[quality]</code> or <code>[resolution]</code>, for example <code>1080p</code> or <code>2160p</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>[source]</code>, for example <code>WEB</code> or <code>BluRay</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>[codec]</code>, for example <code>H264</code>, <code>x265</code> or <code>HEVC</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>[range]</code> or <code>[hdr]</code>, for example <code>HDR</code>, <code>DV</code> or <code>HLG</code>.</li>
                    <li><code>[repeat]</code> or <code>[proper]</code>, for <code>PROPER</code>, <code>REPACK</code> and similar tags.</li>
                    <li><code>[internal]</code> and <code>[multi]</code>, boolean strings <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>.</li>
                </ul>
            </article>
        </div>
        <p>
            During mapping selection the engine mainly has <code>release</code>,
            <code>section</code>, <code>year</code>, <code>group</code>, <code>quality</code> and
            <code>source</code>. During final site filtering it can also use the richer media keys
            above.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>Rule priority</h2>
        <p>
            This order matters. A normal <code>ALLOW</code> does not override a matching
            <code>DROP</code>. Use <code>EXCEPT</code> when you need a whitelist carve-out.
        </p>
<pre class="code">1. Section EXCEPT rules
2. Mapping EXCEPT rules for the selected CBFTP mapping
3. Section DROP rules
4. Mapping DROP rules for the selected CBFTP mapping
5. Mapping ALLOW rules for the selected CBFTP mapping
6. Section ALLOW rules
7. Default: ALLOW</pre>
        <p>
            Section rules apply to every mapping under that IRC section. Mapping rules apply
            only to one CBFTP mapping.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>Section rules vs mapping rules</h2>
        <div class="doc-grid compact">
            <article class="doc-card">
                <h3>Section Rules</h3>
                <p>
                    Use these for broad filters that should affect every CBFTP mapping under
                    the selected IRC section.
                </p>
<pre class="code">[release] contains INTERNAL DROP
[release] matches \b(FRENCH|GERMAN|DUTCH)\b DROP
[group] isin BadGroup1,BadGroup2 DROP</pre>
            </article>
            <article class="doc-card">
                <h3>CBFTP Mapping Rules</h3>
                <p>
                    Use these when only one mapping needs a special rule, for example a
                    2160p mapping, a strict group list, or a site-specific quality route.
                </p>
<pre class="code">[quality] == 2160p ALLOW
[codec] contains HEVC ALLOW
[release] contains 720p DROP</pre>
            </article>
        </div>
        <p>
            If a section has two mappings, the mapping rules on the first mapping do not
            affect the second mapping. Section rules affect both.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>Common rule recipes</h2>
        <div class="doc-grid compact">
            <article class="doc-card">
                <h3>Language drops</h3>
<pre class="code">[release] matches \bGERMAN\b DROP
[release] matches \bFRENCH\b DROP
[release] matches \bPOLISH\b DROP</pre>
                <p>
                    Word boundaries avoid matching unrelated words. Regex is already
                    case-insensitive.
                </p>
            </article>
            <article class="doc-card">
                <h3>Wildcard drops</h3>
<pre class="code">[release] iswm *.SUBFRENCH.* DROP
[release] iswm *CAM* DROP
[release] iswm *S??E??* ALLOW</pre>
                <p>
                    <code>iswm</code> matches the whole string, so include <code>*</code> on both
                    sides when you mean "contains".
                </p>
            </article>
            <article class="doc-card">
                <h3>Group whitelist</h3>
<pre class="code">[group] isin APPROVED1,APPROVED2 EXCEPT
[release] matches .* DROP</pre>
                <p>
                    Use <code>EXCEPT</code> for the allowed groups. A plain <code>ALLOW</code>
                    would not beat the broad <code>DROP</code>.
                </p>
            </article>
            <article class="doc-card">
                <h3>Quality routing</h3>
<pre class="code">Mapping to TV-2160P trigger: 2160p|UHD|HEVC
Mapping to TV-1080P trigger: 1080p|H264|x264
Fallback mapping trigger: empty</pre>
                <p>
                    Put the specific mapping triggers first and the empty fallback last.
                </p>
            </article>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>Real mapping example</h2>
        <p>
            Two sites can announce different IRC sections but still race into the same
            shared CBFTP section.
        </p>
<pre class="code">Site Alpha:
  IRC section: X264-HD-1080P
  CBFTP mapping: MOVIES-HD
  Section Rules:
    [release] contains 720p DROP
    [release] contains CAM DROP

Site Beta:
  IRC section: X264-HD
  CBFTP mapping: MOVIES-HD
  Section Rules:
    [release] contains CAM DROP</pre>
        <p>
            Result: both sites send accepted releases to CBFTP section <code>MOVIES-HD</code>,
            but Site Alpha drops 720p while Site Beta does not. CBFTP still handles the
            real per-site paths inside its own site/section config.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-grid">
        <article class="doc-card">
            <div class="card-title">PREBOTS</div>
            <p>
                PreBots have their own page. <strong>Add prebot</strong> creates the ZNC and parsing
                config. Paste a real pre line into <strong>Test parsing</strong> to confirm the
                captured section and release before saving. <strong>Import releases</strong> imports
                the latest 100 releases from predb.club into the pretime database.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
            <div class="card-title">PRE MANAGER</div>
            <p>
                The Pre page restores the old Affil Spread &amp; Pre Manager flow: fetch CBFTP sites,
                configure affil directories and sections, refresh releases, distribute with FXP,
                check completion, delete releases and send SITE PRE.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
            <div class="card-title">FXP CLIENT</div>
            <p>
                The FXP Client is the dual-pane CBFTP browser. Pick source and destination sites,
                browse paths, select items, queue left-to-right or right-to-left FXP jobs, and delete
                selected entries when needed.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
            <div class="card-title">CHAT</div>
            <p>
                Chat uses separate IRC connections from the trader. Connecting chat does not start
                racing, and stopping the trader does not disconnect chat.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
            <div class="card-title">LOGS</div>
            <p>
                Logs are split by Application, IRC, CBFTP and Race. Use Logs for live diagnosis,
                and use Test release to confirm why a release would race or be skipped.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
            <div class="card-title">RELEASE HISTORY</div>
            <p>
                The History page stores race events in <code>history/race_history.json</code>
                under the data folder, so Dashboard races seen survives restarts. Reset it
                from Settings when you want a clean counter.
            </p>
        </article>

        <article class="doc-card">
            <div class="card-title">REMOTE ACCESS</div>
            <p>
                By default the WebUI listens on loopback only. Use Settings to change the
                bind address and port. For HTTPS, keep RaceTrade local and use a reverse proxy
                in front of it.
            </p>
        </article>
    </section>

    <section class="doc-section">
        <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
        <ul class="doc-list">
            <li>If a site does not race, check that its IRC section is enabled in the Sections tab.</li>
            <li>If CBFTP says a section is disabled, compare the IRC section and CBFTP mapping names.</li>
            <li>If chat joins channels on the wrong network, check the ZNC Network field and channel list.</li>
            <li>If settings seem missing, confirm the data folder under Settings.</li>
            <li>If old passwords or Blowfish keys fail, migrate legacy <code>ENC:</code> secrets on the original Windows account.</li>
            <li>If the app will not start, another RaceTrade may already be using the configured port.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>
</div>
